[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Dependency Injection in .NET: Lifetimes, Scopes, and Captive Dependencies"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Dependency Injection (DI) is a core architectural pattern in .NET. It promotes loosely coupled code, making applications more maintainable and testable. However, understanding service lifetimes and avoiding architectural pitfalls like captive dependencies is critical for building robust backend systems."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding Service Lifetimes"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The .NET built-in IoC container supports three primary service lifetimes. Choosing the correct lifetime ensures resource efficiency and prevents memory leaks."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Transient (AddTransient): Created each time they are requested. Best for lightweight, stateless services."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Scoped (AddScoped): Created once per client request (e.g., HTTP request). Ideal for Entity Framework contexts and unit-of-work patterns."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Singleton (AddSingleton): Created the first time they are requested and live for the entire application lifecycle. Used for caching or configuration."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Registering Services in Program.cs"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here is how you register different lifetimes using the built-inIServiceCollection interface."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// Registering different service lifetimes
builder.Services.AddTransient<ITransientService, TransientService>();
builder.Services.AddScoped<IScopedService, ScopedService>();
builder.Services.AddSingleton<ISingletonService, SingletonService>();

var app = builder.Build();"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Explicit Service Resolution and Scopes"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Sometimes you need to resolve services manually, particularly in background tasks (hosted services) where a scoped service is needed within a singleton context. You must explicitly create anIServiceScope to safely resolve scoped services."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"public class CustomBackgroundWorker : BackgroundService
{
    private readonly IServiceProvider _serviceProvider;

    public CustomBackgroundWorker(IServiceProvider serviceProvider)
    {
        _serviceProvider = serviceProvider;
    }

    protected override async Task ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken stoppingToken)
    {
        using (var scope = _serviceProvider.CreateScope())
        {
            var scopedService = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<IScopedService>();
            await scopedService.ProcessDataAsync();
        }
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"4. The Danger of Captive Dependencies"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"A captive dependency occurs when a service with a longer lifetime (like a Singleton) holds a reference to a service with a shorter lifetime (like a Scoped service). This traps the scoped service inside the singleton, causing it to live forever and potentially causing memory leaks or accessing disposed database contexts."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"// BAD DESIGN: Singleton capturing a Scoped service
public class BadSingletonService
{
    private readonly IScopedService _scopedService;

    public BadSingletonService(IScopedService scopedService)
    {
        _scopedService = scopedService; // Captive dependency created here!
    }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":".NET has built-in validation checks (enabled by default in Development mode via UseDefaultServiceProvider) that will throw an InvalidOperationException if a singleton attempts to consume a scoped service."}]
